<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zipaquirá</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zipaquirá is a town north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> in Cundinamarca, Colombia, built over large rock-salt deposits. Under the Muisca it was a major source of salt, a staple of their trade, and the Spanish continued to exploit its mines. It is known today for its Salt Cathedral, an underground church carved in the old mine galleries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zipaquira.jpg" alt="Zipaquirá" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Mahecha · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plaza_principal_de_Zipaquir%C3%A1_2014-09-05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
